--- ANSWER ---
**Review of the c116d J2 failure: the "reference table ran out" reading is probably right, but the op's own output can't prove it. The ~63-call second attempt is not shown to be safe.**

**1. Strongest reason the reading could be wrong**

The error 1055s are not evidence of reference exhaustion. They only show that the Traverse's output stopped being usable.
- In `OpWireJoints_v0` the Property Node's `error in` is not wired. `tools/bench/diag_c116d_opbuild.log:27` shows terminal 2, `error in (no error)`, with wire 0, and the op reports `error out 2` of that node.
- So the op throws away the Traverse's own error. Any Traverse failure reaches you as error 1055 with uid echo 0: real memory exhaustion, reference exhaustion, or an index past the end of a shortened array.
- The Traverse's actual error for reads 536 and later was discarded. What it probably was only shows up afterwards, in `report_all(GObject)`'s error 2 (`diag_c116d_j3.log:88`).

The 2^20 limit is also not established for this kind of reference.
- The only evidence I found is a forum measurement on data value references (DVRs): LabVIEW fails at the 1,048,577th, and rolfk explains it as a ~20-bit index into a table kept *per reference type*.
- Nobody there measured VI Server GObject references. See [NI forum, DVR limitation p.2](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Limitation-of-data-value-references-DVR-in-system/td-p/3620115/page/2).
- NI's own KB only says error 2 can come from opening new references ([KB kA00Z0000019KhWSAU](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z0000019KhWSAU)). I could not fetch that page because of a certificate error, so this is from the search snippet only.

**2. Alternative explanations**

- **(a) Real memory, not a count limit.** Private bytes grew by an amount that fits either reading, so this measurement cannot separate them. It is weakened by the fact that LabVIEW 2026 is installed under `Program Files`, not `(x86)`, so it is 64-bit and 666 MB is far from any real limit. That bitness is my inference from the install path.
- **(b) The index, not the call count.** The sweep reads wires in increasing Traverse order. So "every read from call 536 on" is the same set as "every index from about 500 on", and the two cannot be told apart. This is partly answered by the 37 target reads, which hit all index ranges and all passed. But that only rules it out for the early calls.
- **(c) A partly filled array.** Suppose the table filled up in the middle of call 536: the elements below some index would be valid and the rest invalid. That is still exhaustion, just a different shape, and your 1055 evidence fits it equally well.

**3. The evidence in your favour, stated fairly**

- **The numbers fit to within 1%.** 536 × 1,944 leaked references per call = 1,041,984. The gap to 2^20 is 6,592. The work before the J2 read accounts for about 5.2k of that:
  - the census classes add up to 3,213 (`j3.log:43`);
  - the J2 phase's own `report_all(Wire)` adds 1,945.
- **The kernel handle count really does not see these references.** Handles at executor start were 45,690 here (`j3.log:85`) and 45,704 in the 116-2 run without the sweep (`diag_c116b_scratch.log:41`).
- **Private bytes grew as a leak would predict.** They were 665.9 MB here against 574.4 MB in 116-2 at the same point. That extra 91.5 MB is about 88 bytes for each of the ~1.04 M leaked references.

On balance I think the claim holds. What would change my mind is the falsifying test below coming out the other way.

**4. What would falsify it**

Use a fresh LabVIEW, the bed, and `wire_joints` with a **fixed `index=0`** in a loop.
- **If the reading is right:** failure comes at call ≈ (2^20 − P0)/1,944. P0 is whatever the setup leaks; with the ~5.2k from section 3 that is about call 537. After the failure, even a tiny `report_all(Diagram)` (173 objects) should return error 2.
- **It is falsified if:** index 0 never fails past about 600 calls. That would point to (b). It is also falsified if the failure call number follows private bytes rather than calls × matched objects. That would point to (a).

**5. Is ~63 calls safe? Not shown. The recipe's own reads use the same budget.**

- **Each `read_live` is two whole-VI Traverses with nothing closed:**
  - `report_all(GObject)`, about 10,138 references;
  - `OpAllTerms_v1`, 5,811 rows (`docs/toolkit-capabilities.md:346`).
- **Minimum cost:** 26 reads × ~16k ≈ 0.41 M. That assumes only the Traverse arrays leak.
- **It may be much more.** Owner and Diagram columns come from properties that return a new reference per row, and those may leak too.
- **What 116-2 shows:** that run grew +77 MB of private bytes over its 25 reads (574.4 → 651.6 MB, `diag_c116b_scratch.log:41,274`). At the ~88 bytes per reference seen in J2, that is about 0.9 M references. Private bytes are not a clean reference counter, so treat this as a warning sign, not a count.
- **The only hard bound:** 116-2 finished, so the recipe alone uses less than 2^20. Your attempt adds about 0.13 M on top:
  - 63 calls × 1,944;
  - two `report_all(Wire)` calls.
- **The after-rows read is the risky part.** It runs after all 25 recipe reads, when the most budget has already been used. If the recipe alone is above ~0.92 M, the second attempt fails in exactly the same way.
- **Pass the index.** `wire_joints` must get `index=` from one `report_all(Wire)` per phase, as `diag_c116d_j3.py:31,37` does. Without it, `_uid_index` does another 1,945-reference Traverse on every call, which doubles the cost. Also set `sweep=False` in both phases.

**6. Cheapest test that also answers section 5**

Before the attempt, spend about two minutes of LabVIEW on the fixed-index loop from section 4, and log private bytes every 50 calls. Then call `read_live` repeatedly on the bed until error 2, or cap it at 30 calls.

That gives you two numbers:
- whether the 2^20 per-call arithmetic holds;
- how many references each `read_live` really uses. Reads-to-failure × per-read cost ≈ 2^20.

If each `read_live` uses more than about 35k, the planned attempt will run out at the after-rows read. In that case, move that read to a fresh LabVIEW instance or close the Traverse array before spending the last try.

Sources:
- [NI forum – Limitation of DVRs (2^20, per-type table)](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Limitation-of-data-value-references-DVR-in-system/td-p/3620115/page/2)
- [NI KB – Error 2: Memory is Full when Opening a New Reference](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z0000019KhWSAU)
- [NI forum – refnums are magic cookies](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/How-to-get-the-Reference-Number-from-an-User-Event-Refnum/td-p/4006671)
- [NI KB – Error 1055 from a Property or Invoke Node](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z0000019L75SAE)
